  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/16d3d69b6fe4f9db",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-10-05T17:27:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/stories/story/desi-dhamaka-celebrating-community-culture-and-connection-at-your-nac"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Desi Dhamaka: Celebrating community, culture and connection at your NAC"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Your National Arts Centre came alive with the sounds, colours and energy of the second edition of Desi Dhamaka, a vibrant celebration of South Asian culture filled with music, dance, a community market and opportunities to connect and celebrate together. Community members, artists and organizations shared their knowledge and creativity, helping shape an experience that felt welcoming, meaningful and rooted in community. By working alongside community partners and listening to the people we strive to welcome, we can create experiences that reflect the cultures, perspectives and stories of the people who walk through our doors. The relationships continue Community engagement is not about a single event. It is about building trust over time, creating opportunities to work together and making your NAC a place where more people can feel seen, represented and at home. Looking back at Desi Dhamaka gives us an opportunity to recognize the artists, community members, partners, organizations, local businesses and guests who made the day what it was, while also celebrating the relationships that continue beyond the event itself. More than a place to see a performance Desi Dhamaka invited people to experience their NAC as a place to gather, celebrate culture, discover something new and feel at home. The community market brought local businesses and organizations into the heart of the celebration, while performances and activities throughout the afternoon filled the NAC with energy. For some guests, it was also an opportunity to discover the NAC for the first time. Creating experiences like Desi Dhamaka is an important part of how we build relationships with communities. The celebration itself may last an afternoon, but the connections, conversations and partnerships built through it can continue long after. Watch the video and look back at Desi Dhamaka at your NAC."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/22fe097016d16e05",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-10-02T11:00:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/stories/story/neige-sur-abidjan-representing-absence"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Neige sur Abidjan: Representing absence"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Some projects take shape within a few months, while others are carried by an artist for years, evolving along the way until they find their way to the stage almost naturally. Iannicko N’Doua’s Neige sur Abidjan, directed by Marc Beaupré and co-produced by NAC French Theatre, Orange Noyée, and the Centre du Théâtre d’Aujourd’hui, falls into that second category. Iannicko N’Doua and Mani Soleymanlou, artistic director of NAC French Theatre and Orange Noyée, look back on the genesis of this autobiographical play, from the very first draft to the stage premiere. The story begins with an absence: that of a father whom the artist never knew, either as a child or as an adult, until a pivotal encounter. From journey to story In 2009, Iannicko N’Doua travelled to Côte d’Ivoire for the first time to meet his father, who had returned to his native country while Iannicko was still very young. That journey inspired Iannicko’s first writing project. Some dozen pages long, it sat in his desk drawer for almost 10 years. A draft of the project resurfaced in 2014, when Iannicko was cast in the first version of Mani Soleymanlou’s play Un. Deux. Trois. The 40-odd artists involved in the show were invited to participate in a collaborative writing process. Iannicko contributed a short monologue about his relationship with his identity, caught between Quebec and Côte d’Ivoire. His text was short, but it made a strong impression. During the COVID-19 pandemic, as part of Free Rein, a Canada Council for the Arts initiative to support artists through creative residencies, Mani encouraged Iannicko to revisit this material and spend some time on it. At that stage, Iannicko still considered the project to be essentially a piece of writing. He didn’t necessarily envision performing it; the material was too personal. He initially chose to explore it as a writer, without imagining himself sharing his story with an audience. Gradually, however, the piece evolved into a performance. How can absence be portrayed? Initially, Neige sur Abidjan was conceived as a solo piece. But the creative team was faced with a challenge: how can absence be portrayed? It was in response to this question that Hamadoun Kassogué, a legendary figure in Malian theatre and film, joined the project. Initially, Iannicko envisaged Hamadoun’s presence as that of a silent father, a physical embodiment of this absence. Then something changed. After working with the team for several weeks, Hamadoun gradually assumed a different role in the production. The character became a source of wisdom: the griot, guardian of memory and family history. He became the one who knows—about the ancestors, past events, and the invisible bonds that connect generations. The one who accompanies Iannicko on his quest, and also guides the audience through the story. So the figure of the father doesn’t disappear: it is transformed. Absence becomes presence, then transmission. To tackle this deeply personal subject matter, Iannicko wanted to work with someone in whom he had absolute artistic and personal trust. That person was Marc Beaupré, the first director Iannicko had worked with after his graduation from the National Theatre School. Together, they explored how to translate memory, family, and absence into a compelling piece of theatre. A show that keeps evolving Neige sur Abidjan is not a static story. Even so, the process of transforming an intimate experience into a work of art is inevitably therapeutic for the creator, providing a means of processing pain and expressing absence, and opening up a space for reconciliation. The production itself continues to change over time. “Every time I come back to it, I rework it based on where I am in my life,” explains Iannicko N’Doua. NAC French Theatre is delighted to present Neige sur Abidjan, October 8 to 10, 2026, in the Azrieli Studio."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/b03f5d8eefce102e",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-09-21T11:30:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/stories/story/reconcilethis-pairs-indigenous-crafts-and-performance-in-final-season"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "#ReconcileThis pairs Indigenous crafts and performance in final season"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "#ReconcileThis closes its multi-year journey the way it began: by putting Indigenous voices at the centre of the story. For its fourth and final phase, the project turns to art forms that carry story but rarely share the spotlight with performance. The Fire Keepers This year's phase was produced by RJ Mitchell, a Mohawk of Akwesasne and member of the Bear Clan, who joined NAC Indigenous Theatre after working in recording studios and live sound, including a stint at the Aultsville Theatre in Cornwall, Ontario. Mitchell had previously worked with the department as Assistant Technical Director during the launch of NAC Indigenous Theatre in 2019. For this phase, Mitchell partnered Indigenous performance artists with artisans, Indigenous creators who tell stories through physical media rather than the stage. Together, these pairs made up the Fire Keepers. \"Cultural artists who work in physical media have been telling stories for as long as performers, but how can they tell those stories in an online format?\" \"Cultural artists who work in physical media have been telling stories for as long as performers,\" Mitchell says, \"but how can they tell those stories in an online format?\" That question became the foundation for the phase. Each material artist created a piece that carried a story. A performer then interpreted and brought that piece to life through movement, song, or dance. Creations from coast to coast to coast From Nunavut, Koomuatuk Sapa Curley (Kuzy) and Donovan Gordon-Tootoo brought the North to the Ottawa Valley. Kuzy carved a bear from stone while Donovan's drum recalled the sounds of home. From the Mohawk community of Akwesasne, Carrie Hill and Feryn King paired basket weaving with hoop dancing. As Carrie worked black ash splints into a basket, Feryn's hoop movements echoed the same weaving technique in motion. From British Columbia, Raven Grenier, Bill Barnes, and Adanchilla Pauls Lepine came together around Wolverine, a performance created by Grenier. Pauls Lepine beaded a formline heart as its centrepiece, while Barnes and Grenier worked decorative chains into the performers' outfits and hairstyling. Wolverine was performed by Grenier and Aysha Alfred, telling a story passed down for generations on the West Coast. From unceded Algonquin territory, sisters Mairi and Claire Brascoupé of Kitigan Zibi shared a piece rooted in the land itself. Mairi harvested and folded birch bark before biting intricate patterns into it, while Claire's singing and drumming accompanied her, a shared practice that spoke directly to the history of those who have stewarded this territory, including the land Ottawa now sits on. From the East Coast, newlyweds Kelly Sark and Richard Lush brought Mi'kmaq Kojua tradition to the project. Sark created the regalia that Lush wore as he performed the Kojua dance. \"I'm hoping to bring a more wholistic view on storytelling, one that doesn't necessarily rely on words and voices but can be told through our hands.\" Storytelling with our hands Each pairing reflected Mitchell's goal for the phase: to prove that performance art and material art have more in common than they have differences, and to give artisans, many of whom have less footing in online spaces, a platform alongside their performing collaborators. \"I'm hoping to bring a more wholistic view on storytelling,\" Mitchell says, \"one that doesn't necessarily rely on words and voices but can be told through our hands.\" Mitchell traces the idea back to a conversation with Lori Marchand, Managing Director of NAC Indigenous Theatre, who encouraged Mitchell early on to keep building space for the next generation. That same instinct shaped the final year of #ReconcileThis. A four-year journey Since its launch in 2021, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling, in partnership with Meta. The first year, led by Paula Chinkiwsky, introduced the Fire Starters. Artists created digital pieces responding to the question: what does reconciliation mean to you? The work culminated in Papakanje, a live performance featuring the Fire Starters on the NAC's Babs Asper Theatre stage. In year two, Anishinaabe playwright and producer Josh Languedoc led the Fire Carriers, pairing established Indigenous artists with younger collaborators for a collaboration built on mentorship and cross-generational exchange, looking at both history and Indigenous futures. The third year, led by Red River Michif and Jewish theatre artist Philip Geller, became Braiding Generations: Visits of Joy and Mischief. Drawing on Trickster teachings, the Fire Tenders recorded conversations with family, Elders, and kin, work later reinterpreted by Indigenous video artists. Now, with the Fire Keepers, the project is closing its four-year run by turning to the hands that shape material art, and the performers who helped those stories speak."
      }
    ]
  },
