  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/b295b6a65f00af65",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/42122/rt-trailer-web-thumbnail__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-09-16T09:33:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/reconcile-this-2026-trailer"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "#ReconcileThis 2026 trailer"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Over the last four years, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling. Led by NAC Indigenous Theatre, the project is a space where reconciliation is carried through art, where communities are uplifted, and where Indigenous stories live online. For the fourth year of #ReconcileThis, produced and curated by RJ Mitchell, Indigenous performance artists partnered with artisans to share stories representing the diverse cultures of Turtle Island, with artists from the West Coast, the Maritimes, the North, and the Ottawa Valley. Dancers, singers, and drummers worked alongside beaders, carvers, and weavers to create and share. This project is made possible thanks to the generous support of Meta."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/cc5067badb5beec4",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/42041/resonance__large.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-09-09T14:17:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/bassoon-resonance-exercises"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Less work, better sound: Resonance exercises for bassoon"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "In this video, Andrew Brady (Principal Bassoon, Saint Paul Chamber Orchestra and Los Angeles Chamber Orchestra) works with Darren Hicks (Principal Bassoon, National Arts Centre Orchestra) on developing resonance to project sound efficiently in orchestral playing&mdash;without forcing or fatigue. Drawing from vocal techniques and overtone singing, Andrew demonstrates how small changes in tongue position, vowel shape, and resonance focus can dramatically increase clarity, projection, and color while maintaining a centered core sound. Chapters 00:21 Why bassoonists struggle to play loud in an orchestra 01:21 How to get a bigger sound without fatigue 02:02 The bassoon&rsquo;s role: Blending vs cutting through 03:00 Vocal techniques for better bassoon resonance 03:56 Singing intervals to improve resonance (drone exercise) 04:54 Overtones, nasal resonance and sound projection 05:38 Demonstration: Overtone singing for bassoonists 07:07 Applying vocal resonance to the bassoon 08:32 Tongue placement and controlling overtones 09:24 Maintaining resonance across intervals 10:30 Vowel shapes that increase projection 11:33 Resonance first, intonation second 12:46 Balancing core sound and upper overtones 14:01 What resonance feels like when playing 15:25 How balanced sound projects in a concert hall 16:52 Scale demo: Weak vs resonant bassoon sound 18:14 Playing less but sounding bigger 19:25 Expanding your bassoon tone colour palette 20:40 Musical applications (Firebird, orchestral solos) 21:27 Conclusion and next lesson (support and vibrato)&nbsp; Be sure to check out the companion video on support and vibrato for more performance fundamentals."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/d987bb75bd63b084",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/42084/support_and_vibrato__large.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-09-09T14:00:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/bassoon-support-and-vibrato"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Bassoon support and vibrato explained"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Andrew Brady (Principal Bassoon, Saint Paul Chamber Orchestra and Los Angeles Chamber Orchestra) and Darren Hicks (Principal Bassoon, National Arts Centre Orchestra) break down support and vibrato. This lesson focuses on abdominal engagement, expansion, and efficiency, showing how support should feel, how it functions musically, and how it directly enables a controlled, connected vibrato. Chapters&nbsp; 0:52 &ndash; Why tension kills sound:&nbsp;How tension reduces resonance, volume, and overtones. 2:23 &ndash; The &ldquo;Ho!&rdquo; exercise for finding support:&nbsp;Using a physical, vocal gesture to feel outward abdominal engagement. 5:03 &ndash; Shifting engagement for legato:&nbsp; Adjusting support timing to accommodate finger movement and resistance. 6:29 &ndash; Playing with maximum support and&nbsp;legato:&nbsp;Maintaining steady engagement across the scale. 7:25 &ndash; Vibrato as amplitude, not pitch:&nbsp;Why this approach avoids pitch wobble and excess tension. 8:09 &ndash; Subdivision-based vibrato practice:&nbsp;Using triplets and rhythmic pulsation to train vibrato control. 8:39 &ndash; Adding vibrato to alternate notes:&nbsp;Maintaining legato while introducing vibrato gradually. 10:50 &ndash; Increasing subdivisions (16ths, quintuplets, etc.):&nbsp;Building vibrato flexibility across speeds. 11:38 &ndash; Building strength and&nbsp;endurance:&nbsp;Why vibrato training can feel like an abdominal workout. 13:06 &ndash; Advanced vibrato control: Switching vibrato speeds seamlessly while maintaining legato. 15:02 &ndash; Vibrato depth vs. speed:&nbsp;How faster vibrato naturally reduces amplitude. 15:30 &ndash; Subtle vs. wide vibrato: Exploring different vibrato widths for colour and style. 16:09 &ndash; Performance: Wolfgang Amadeus MOZART Sonata for Bassoon and Cello, K.292 If you enjoyed this lesson, be sure to check out the companion video on resonance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/4155d1e27622ec9c",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/41950/vias__large.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-08-13T15:25:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/as-long-as-we-exist-vias"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "As long as we exist "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "An NAC Dance commission The duo V&iacute;as &mdash; composed of Diana Le&oacute;n and Paco Ziel &mdash; engage in an intimate conversation through the body, surrendering to the paradoxical emotional diversity we face in today&#39;s world. Inhabiting the space of the NAC Salon, this dance invites us to navigate through the denial of pain, acceptance and the thrill of being alive. &ldquo;Moments like the one we experienced while dancing As Long as We Exist at the NAC are the reason we do what we do. This duet is an open window into our intimacy. How did we get here? Who are we now, and where are we today? How does our relationship relate to the world, and how do we empathize and act towards its injustices together? Face to face, we walk on a journey of emotional complexity, letting ourselves fall into one another to find ground and hold on to the ecstasy of being alive.&rdquo;&nbsp;&ndash; Paco Ziel and Diana Le&oacute;n Credits Choreography and interpretation:&nbsp;Paco Ziel and Diana Le&oacute;n Costume design:&nbsp;Siam Obreg&oacute;n Rehearsal direction:&nbsp;Alyssa Allen Music:&nbsp;Franz Schubert Schubert: Wiegenlied, D. 867.&nbsp;Performed by Wolfgang Holzmair and Imogen Cooper (Universal Music Group) Thekla (eine Geisterstimme) Op. 88 2, D. 595. Performed by Riko Fukuda and Jasper Schweppe (Etcetera Records) Willkommen und Abschied, D. 767. Performed by Wolfgang Holzmair and Gerard Wys (Naxos of America) Video: Samuel Coombes"
      }
    ]
  },
