    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Kojua regalia and dancing"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Representing First Nations from the East Coast, newlyweds Kelly Sark and Richard Lush brought their love for their culture to #ReconcileThis through a traditional Kojua dance. Kelly created the regalia that Richard proudly wore while he danced in the Kojua style, beside serene water and under blue skies. Credits Artist: Kelly Sark Dancer: Richard Lush #ReconcileThis Over the last four years, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling. Led by NAC Indigenous Theatre, the project is a space where reconciliation is carried through art, where communities are uplifted, and where Indigenous stories live online. For the fourth year of #ReconcileThis, produced and curated by RJ Mitchell, Indigenous performance artists partnered with artisans to share stories representing the diverse cultures of Turtle Island, with artists from the West Coast, the Maritimes, the North, and the Ottawa Valley. Dancers, singers, and drummers worked alongside beaders, carvers, and weavers to create and share."
      }
    ]
  },
