    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Birchbark biting and singing"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Sisters Mairi and Claire Brascoupe from Kitigan Zibi shared their connection to the land from unceded Algonquin Territory. Mairi harvested birchbark, folding pieces before biting intricate patterns into them, and Claire sang while drumming. It was a shared experience that spoke directly to the history of those who lived and stewarded the land that Ottawa currently sits on. Credits Artist: Mairi Brascoupe Singer: Claire Brascoupe #ReconcileThis Over the last four years, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling. Led by NAC Indigenous Theatre, the project is a space where reconciliation is carried through art, where communities are uplifted, and where Indigenous stories live online. For the fourth year of #ReconcileThis, produced and curated by RJ Mitchell, Indigenous performance artists partnered with artisans to share stories representing the diverse cultures of Turtle Island, with artists from the West Coast, the Maritimes, the North, and the Ottawa Valley. Dancers, singers, and drummers worked alongside beaders, carvers, and weavers to create and share."
      }
    ]
