      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "&ldquo;Collaboration is the highest goal in creating art, because you&rsquo;re taking the best of the best minds. And we&rsquo;re each contributing something that&rsquo;s bigger than the sum of us.&rdquo; Laakkuluk Williamson, Co-Creator and Director, and Vinnie Karetak, Co-Creator and Performer, Qaumma&#8239;&#8239;&#8239; In this interview, Laakkuluk Williamson and Vinnie Karetak discuss Qaumma, a production that pays tribute to Inuit women, their resilience in the face of colonization and the light they carry within them. The co-creators highlight the pivotal role played by the National Arts Centre&rsquo;s National Creation Fund, which gave them a foundation for the creative process and supported an artistic collaboration that celebrates Indigenous voices.&nbsp;&nbsp; Qaumma is one of more than 117 works supported by the Fund, and it was presented at the NAC as part of Indigenous Theatre&rsquo;s 2024&ndash;2025 season.&nbsp;&nbsp; The Fund empowers Canadian artists to embrace creative risks, explore new forms and make their most ambitious dreams a reality, resulting in innovative performances that are enjoyed by audiences across Canada and abroad.&nbsp;&nbsp; The National Creation Fund is powered by philanthropy. Thank you to our donors for making it possible.&nbsp;&nbsp; To learn more about the National Creation Fund: https://nac-cna.ca/en/creationfund&nbsp;"
      }
    ]
  },
