    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/indigenous-cities-montreal-napatat-soleil-launiere"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Napatat"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Soleil Launi&egrave;re intertwines notions of two-spirit body while drawing inspiration from cosmogony and the Innu world. Working with memory holder &Eacute;toile du matin, Napatat honours sacred connections through roots, a performance piece creating a medicine path as a healing process through earth and souls. Location:&nbsp;Des Royaux Park, Montreal, Quebec Credits&nbsp; Production AUEN presents Napatat A performance piece by Soleil Launi&egrave;re&nbsp; Based on a memory from &Eacute;toile du matin of the Deer Clan&nbsp; Directed and edited by Antoine Amnotte-Dupuis&nbsp; &nbsp; Indigenous Cities: The stories here. Experience stories from the cities you thought you knew &ndash; Montreal, Vancouver, Saskatoon, and Ottawa. Indigenous Cities presents memories from Indigenous community members based in cities across Canada; memories that have been interpreted by Indigenous artists to create a unique audio storytelling experience.&nbsp; Learn more:&nbsp;Indigenous Cities Montreal&nbsp;"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/b578bce17b07329d",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/41392/salesman_in_china-stratford_festival_2024-credit-david-hou__large.png"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-04-30T10:49:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/national-creation-fund-salesman-in-china"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Celebrating the voices of Asian Canadian artists"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "&ldquo;When I began my career over 30 years ago, Asian Canadians were kind of relegated to the small studios and back spaces. [Salesman in China marks] an extraordinary change.&rdquo; Jovanni Sy, Writer and Director, Salesman in China In this interview, playwrights Leanna Brodie and Jovanni Sy talk about Salesman in China, and how both spoken Mandarin and subtitling are woven into it. They reflect on the growth achievements of artists of Asian Canadian artists, and highlight the crucial role played by the National Arts Centre&rsquo;s National Creation Fund in helping the play grace major stages nationwide.&nbsp;&nbsp; Salesman in China by the Stratford Festival is one of more than 115 works supported by the Fund, and it was presented at the NAC as part of English Theatre&rsquo;s 2024&ndash;2025 season.&nbsp; The Fund empowers Canadian artists to embrace creative risks, explore new forms and make their most ambitious dreams a reality, resulting in innovative performances that are enjoyed by audiences across Canada and abroad.&nbsp; The National Creation Fund is powered by philanthropy. Thank you to our donors for making it possible.&nbsp;"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/ae108a4c194bc844",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/41387/idd-2025-video-01.00_00_20_18-still006__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-04-28T14:12:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/heart-activation-mouvements-de-curs-international-dance-day-2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "International Dance Day: “Heart Activation”"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "International Dance Day is a time to celebrate the power of movement, expression, and shared experience as the heartbeat that connects us. To mark this special occasion, around 50 NAC employees gathered on the Babs Asper Theatre stage to perform before the cameras a two-minute choreography signed by Dance Teaching Artist Si&ocirc;ned Watkins. Imagined and conceived for the Kipnes Lantern, Heart Activation is meant to inspire anyone passing by the NAC on April 29, and beautifully illustrates how everyone can dance, and why dance is simply vital. &ldquo;From my experience, dance can be healing. I wanted to find interconnectivity through group choreography and team engagement, to share together the joyful intensities of moving to the same beat.&rdquo;&nbsp; Si&ocirc;ned Watkins, NAC Dance Teaching Artist Naturally, this project quickly turned into a team-building experience for employees across 16 different departments at the NAC. They displayed their best dance moves in an incredible spirit of camaraderie. Sofia, the six-year-old special guest, appears in an ever-growing sequence of concentric circles of dancers accompanying her on stage in a celebration of support. This soulful choreography reminds us that we are all interconnected, alive and that our bodies are natural storytellers. On International Dance Day, we recognize the artists who move us&mdash;and move with courage. We honour the audiences who enthusiastically fill the halls, fueled by curiosity and enthusiasm. We applaud the communities who keep dance traditions vibrant and alive, and support the young dancers&mdash;just like Sofia&mdash;who are beginning to discover their rhythm and who dream of one day telling their own stories on stage."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/1c4a94e132e3c591",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/41345/2026-04-22-tomson_highway-yt-thumbnail-02-without_icon__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2026-04-28T10:56:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/tomson-highway-on-rose-beauty-and-the-future-of-indigenous-storytelling"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Tomson Highway on <em>Rose</em>, beauty, and the future of Indigenous storytelling"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "One of the country&rsquo;s most celebrated playwrights, Tomson Highway joins NAC Indigenous Theatre Artistic Director Kevin Loring for a conversation about Rose, the long-awaited third chapter in the acclaimed cycle that began with The Rez Sisters and Dry Lips Oughta Move to Kapuskasing. Highway speaks to the driving force behind his life&rsquo;s work, and where he believes Indigenous storytelling is headed. Officer of the Order of Canada and recipient of 11 honorary doctorates, Highway enjoys an international career as a playwright, novelist, pianist, and songwriter."
      }
    ]
  },
