    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "#ReconcileThis: wîyâtikwêwin (joy)"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Moe Clark&rsquo;s w&icirc;y&acirc;tikw&ecirc;win (Joy) moves between song and spoken word to trace joy, grief, and healing within M&eacute;tis and 2Spirit communities. Through ceremony, language, and connection to land and ancestors, the piece honours Elders who hold teachings and make space for both pain and laughter. At the crossing of rivers where ancestral spaces awaken, communities gather to heal together and reclaim belonging. Credits by moe clark music by moe clark recorded by moe clark + david drury mixed by david drury featuring teachings from 2S michif kohkomwak barbara bruce + charlotte nolin n&ecirc;hiyaw&ecirc;win translation + cultural consultation: joseph naytowhow Photography &copy;&nbsp;2025 Hayf Photography, Medicine Wolf Healing Place (Treaty 1) &nbsp; #ReconcileThis Since 2021, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling. Led by NAC Indigenous Theatre in collaboration with Meta, the project is a space where reconciliation is carried through art, where communities are uplifted, and where Indigenous stories live online. Now, in its third year, #ReconcileThis turns to the theme of joy and mischief. Led by Red River Michif and Jewish theatre artist Philip Geller, this season shares the laughter, teasing, and playful resilience that connect communities across generations."
      }
    ]
  },
