    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "#ReconcileThis: Point Park"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Anishinaabe Two Spirit comedian, journalist, and producer Issa Kixen sat down with their mom, Gail Roach-Leforte, in Winnipeg, Treaty 1 territory, to talk about the value of memory and the ways joy is carried across generations. That intimate visit sparked a new creation: animator Claire Brascoup&eacute; brought their words to life through a short film that reflects how family connections create pathways for healing, and resilience. &nbsp; #ReconcileThis Since 2021, #ReconcileThis has invited Indigenous artists to share their voices and perspectives through digital storytelling. Led by NAC Indigenous Theatre in collaboration with Meta, the project is a space where reconciliation is carried through art, where communities are uplifted, and where Indigenous stories live online.&nbsp; Now, in its third year, #ReconcileThis turns to the theme of joy and mischief. Led by Red River Michif and Jewish theatre artist Philip Geller, this season shares the laughter, teasing, and playful resilience that connect communities across generations."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/beb3422f34e87bc0",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/39688/surveille-et-punie2_credit_samuel-vincent-coombes__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-10-09T15:27:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/fonds-creation-philippe-cyr-surveille-et-punie"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Transforming hate into music"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "&ldquo;With Surveill&eacute;e et punie, we took the hateful comments directed at Safia Nolin and turned them into a music-filled performance. Our main intention was to take that hatred and transform it, as a way of shifting these hateful comments in our society.&rdquo; Philippe Cyr, director of&nbsp;Surveill&eacute;e et punie &nbsp; In this interview, Philippe Cyr talks about Surveill&eacute;e et punie&mdash;a timely topic as we mark World Mental Health Day. He explains how he drew on hatred to transform it, and notes that the support of National Arts Centre&rsquo;s National Creation Fund was a key factor in the project&rsquo;s success.&nbsp;&nbsp; Surveill&eacute;e et punie is one of more than 100 major new creations supported by the Fund. It premiered at Festival TransAm&eacute;riques and was presented at the NAC during the 2024&ndash;25 season, as well as on international stages. &nbsp; The Fund, which has invested in more than 100 major new performances to date, empowers Canadian artists to embrace creative risks, explore new forms and make their most ambitious dreams a reality. The result? Innovative performances lauded by audiences across Canada and abroad."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/c4b0183d18700ef2",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/39791/ncf-raven-mother_still__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-09-29T15:03:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/creation-raven-mother-interview-2025"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Supporting Indigenous creation"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "&ldquo;For our family, artistic creation is a lived practice. The National Creation Fund&rsquo;s support is allowing this art form to take its place among all the art forms of Canada.&rdquo; Margaret Grenier, Artistic director and choreographer, and&nbsp;Andrew Grenier,&nbsp;Production manager, set and design In this interview, Margaret and Andrew Grenier&nbsp;talk about Raven Mother, the revival of Indigenous song and dance, the vital role of mothers in preserving cultural knowledge, and the impact of the support of the National Arts Centre&rsquo;s National Creation Fund.&nbsp; The Fund, which has invested in more than 100 major new performances to date, empowers Canadian artists to embrace creative risks, explore new forms and make their most ambitious dreams a reality. The result? Innovative performances lauded by audiences across Canada and abroad."
      }
    ]
