      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/nac-chef-series-buffalo-carrots-pink-margarita-mcquay-vassard"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Buffalo carrots and a pink margarita: An NAC Chef Series recipe "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Turn up the flavour with this bold and colorful duo&mdash;buffalo-style carrots with a kick, perfectly paired with a refreshing pink margarita! Whether you&#39;re hosting a brunch, game day, or just craving something fun and different, this combo brings the heat and the chill in all the right ways.&nbsp;Featuring 1 Elgin sous-chef Connor McQuay &amp; mixologist Marc-Andr&eacute; Vassard. Recipe:&nbsp;Buffalo Carrots with Blue Cheese Dressing&nbsp;&nbsp; Ingredients&nbsp;(Serves 4-6) For the roasted carrots:&nbsp; 8 large carrots, peeled and cut to your liking&nbsp; 1 tablespoon oil&nbsp; Salt and pepper&nbsp; For the blue cheese dressing (about 2 cups):&nbsp; 5 egg yolks&nbsp; 1 tablespoon Dijon mustard&nbsp; 1 tablespoon red wine vinegar&nbsp; 750 ml canola oil&nbsp; 1 cup buttermilk&nbsp; Juice of 1 lemon (about 2 tablespoons)&nbsp; 1 cup plain unsweetened yogurt&nbsp; 1/2 shallot, finely diced&nbsp; 4 tablespoons chopped chives&nbsp; 4 tablespoons chopped mint&nbsp; 1/2 cup chopped parsley&nbsp; 3 cloves garlic, minced&nbsp; 400 g crumbled blue cheese&nbsp; Salt and black pepper to taste&nbsp; For the carrot ginger hot sauce:&nbsp; 4 teaspoons olive oil&nbsp; 4 large carrots, peeled and diced&nbsp; 4 small onions, chopped&nbsp; 4 habanero peppers, chopped (adjust based on heat preference)&nbsp; 12 garlic cloves, chopped&nbsp; 4 tomatoes, chopped&nbsp; 2 thumbs of ginger, sliced&nbsp; &frac14; cup honey&nbsp; &frac12; cup white wine vinegar&nbsp; 4 cups water&nbsp; Salt and pepper to taste&nbsp; Buffalo sauce:&nbsp; Your favorite hot sauce (about 1 liter)&nbsp; 1 pound butter&nbsp; Garnish (optional):&nbsp; Microgreens, parsley, chives, edible flowers&nbsp; Crispy shallots&nbsp; Instructions Roast the carrots:&nbsp; Toss carrots with oil, salt, and pepper.&nbsp; Roast in a preheated oven at 525&deg;F (275&deg;C) for about 5-7 minutes until charred but still firm.&nbsp;Set aside.&nbsp; Make the blue cheese dressing:&nbsp; In a bowl, whisk egg yolks, mustard, and vinegar.&nbsp; Slowly add oil while whisking until thickened.&nbsp; Stir in buttermilk, lemon juice, yogurt, shallots, garlic, herbs, and blue cheese.&nbsp; Season with salt and pepper to taste.&nbsp;Chill until ready to serve.&nbsp; Prepare the carrot hot sauce:&nbsp; Heat oil in a saucepan over medium heat.&nbsp; Add carrots, onions, and peppers; cook for 7 minutes.&nbsp; Add garlic; cook 1 minute.&nbsp; Add tomatoes and cook until they break down.&nbsp; Add honey, vinegar, water, salt, and pepper.&nbsp; Bring to a boil, then simmer for 20 minutes.&nbsp; Cool, then blend until smooth. Strain to remove solids.&nbsp; Make the buffalo sauce:&nbsp; Mix hot sauce with melted butter (ratio 1:1). About 1 cup of hot sauce for 4-6 servings.&nbsp;&nbsp; Assemble to serve:&nbsp; Warm the roasted carrots.&nbsp; Toss carrots in the buffalo sauce.&nbsp; Plate the blue cheese dressing on the bottom.&nbsp; Top with buffaloed carrots.&nbsp; Garnish with fresh herbs, microgreens, edible flowers, and crispy shallots if desired.&nbsp;Enjoy your flavorful Buffalo Carrots with creamy blue cheese dressing! &nbsp; Recipe: Pink Margarita 1.5 oz tequila&nbsp; 0.5 oz triple sec&nbsp; 1 oz lime juice&nbsp;&nbsp; 1 oz Ube syrup&nbsp; 1 drop Grenadine&nbsp; Shake with ice.&nbsp; Rim the glass with lime juice and then dip in salt.&nbsp;For something spicier try Tanjin seasoning.&nbsp;"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/39308025487ff072",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/38925/nac_chef_series_episode_2.00_03_39_13.still002__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-04-24T09:33:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/nac-chef-series-deep-fried-cod-sounds-ross-larkin"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Discover deep-fried cod sounds: An NAC Chef Series recipe "
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "There is a bounty of wild food and game to explore in Newfoundland cuisine, but deep-fried cod sounds (the swim bladder of the fish!), was something new to NAC Executive Chef Kenton Leier.&nbsp; Resident Chef Ross Larkin demonstrates how to prepare this unique dish from Canada&rsquo;s east coast. If you can&rsquo;t find cod sounds at your local fish market, you can comfort yourself by whipping up a batch of Chef Kenton&rsquo;s Helluva Onion Dip. Make it your own with your favourite herbs, and it will quickly become your new go-to for chips or veggies. Chef Ross Larkin was the Resident Chef from March through May 2025 along with his wife and business partner Chef Celeste Mah. Together they own Portage restaurant in historic downtown St John&rsquo;s, Newfoundland. Recipe: Chef Kenton&rsquo;s Helluva Onion Dip Great for a group of four! Ingredients 1 cup full-fat sour cream &frac14; cup mayonnaise 1 tbsp Dijon mustard &frac12; lemon, juice only &frac34; cup finely chopped onion 1 tbsp chopped capers 2 tbsp fresh chopped herbs (chives, parsley, dill)&nbsp;&nbsp; Salt and pepper to taste Method 1. Cook onions over medium heat slowly in a pan with a small amount of oil until tender and browned. Let cool. 2. In a medium mixing bowl combine all other ingredients and mix well. 3. Place dip in a serving bowl, garnish with fresh herbs, and serve with your favourite chips (or deep-fried cod sounds, if you&#39;re lucky)!"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/584bde9854388679",
    "@type": [
      "http://schema.org/VideoObject"
    ],
    "http://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://naccnaca-videos.s3.amazonaws.com/38739/et-culture_gap_in_the_climate_crisis_-_thumb__large.jpg"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/uploadDate": [
      {
        "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
        "@value": "2025-03-18T10:11:00-04:00"
      }
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/en/video/the-culture-gap-in-the-climate-crisis"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/name": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Culture Gap in the Climate Crisis"
      }
    ],
    "http://schema.org/description": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The great transition currently underway from an extractive society to a regenerative society is most commonly framed in economic, technological and political terms. As significant efforts across these fields struggle to fully activate the transformation, can the arts help to close the gap by activating our cultural selves towards more sustainable lives? In this panel, Climate Dramaturg Vicki Stroich (Caravan Farm Theatre), Metcalf Fellow on Arts and Society David Maggs, and NAC English Theatre&rsquo;s Judi Pearl discuss their emergent, research-based approach to working with artists around themes of climate and sustainability. With Irresistible Neighbourhoods as the primary case study, this talk will explore the conceptual framework underpinning the project, and how the process aims to guide artists away from didactic imperatives and towards more imaginative storytelling, with the ultimate goal of cultivating a sense of belonging for audiences in worlds we are yet to inhabit. Moderated by Nina Lee Aquino."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://nac-cna.ca/genid/7124d423426361c5",
